  "notes": "USAGE:\nSense 1 is a standalone verb meaning to harm or impair. Sense 2 is a grammatical suffix attached to verb stems, meaning to fail at the action. Both are common in everyday Japanese.\n\nVERB SUFFIX PATTERN:\nV-stem + {損|そこ}なう: fail to do V\n- {乗|の}り{損|そこ}なう: to miss (a ride)\n- {言|い}い{損|そこ}なう: to fail to say\n- {食|た}べ{損|そこ}なう: to miss eating\n- {見|み}{損|そこ}なう: to misjudge; to miss seeing\n\nCOMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- {健康|けんこう}を{損|そこ}なう: to harm one's health\n- {信頼|しんらい}を{損|そこ}なう: to damage trust\n- {名誉|めいよ}を{損|そこ}なう: to damage one's honor\n- {品位|ひんい}を{損|そこ}なう: to compromise one's dignity\n- {景観|けいかん}を{損|そこ}なう: to spoil the scenery",
